  "paraId": "6AF68B02",
  "style": "",
  "text": "Our medical facility directs individuals presenting with hip fractures to the ED for an initial evaluation and treatment. Subsequently, the participants were referred to the orthopedic department for surgical intervention. To conduct our retrospective analysis, we used both our hospital’s digital medical record and physical patient files. Patient demographic, clinical, and laboratory data were extracted from the Hospital Information Management System and recorded in a Microsoft Excel spreadsheet. The data set of the study is provided in S1 File. The data for the study were accessed on May 20, 21, and 22, 2024 for research purposes. We narrowed our focus first to individuals diagnosed with hip fractures then femoral neck fractures who had undergone surgical repair. Participants were categorized into two groups: survival and non-survival at 30-day and 1-year mortality edpoints. The power of the HALP score in predicting mortality was compared between the groups using appropriate statistical tests. The Death Notification System of the Ministry of Health was used to determine the 1-year mortality of the participants. In addition, the power of the HALP score in predicting 1-year all-cause mortality in participants was compared with the CRP and CAR. In addition, survival analysis of the HALP score and other indices at optimal thresholds was performed, and the biomarkers were compared with each other."
}

