{
  "paraId": "5D39FF68",
  "style": "",
  "text": "Farag et al. analyzed 32 tumor studies associated with HALP score. According to their results, the HALP score can be used as an independent risk factor to predict poor prognostic outcomes and tumor grading or differentiation [30]. The median HALP score among all tumor subtypes in this review was 31.2, with some cancer subtypes having higher (gastric cancer: 56.8, adenocarcinoma: 44.56) or lower (colorectal: 26.5, bladder: 22.2) median scores. Although there is no standardized and objective threshold that can be valid for all cancers, low HALP scores have been found to be statistically significant for poor outcome in intergroup comparisons in most cancers [6, 31–36]. In our study, the median HALP score was 23.35. This shows the heterogeneity of the HALP score. In addition, the HALP score was significantly lower in the non-survivor group at 30-day and 1-year mortality."
}

